WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.520
If you've been active in the aimtraining community&nbsp;
at any point over the past... 4 years or so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.720
something like this should be pretty familiar to&nbsp;
you. These are the voltaic benchmarks and going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.120
back as far as I can remember these have been&nbsp;
considered the default way to test your skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.360
when training. Over time they have taken&nbsp;
on a bit of a different role though, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.120
the benchmarks themselves becoming the training&nbsp;
tool, not just something to test your skill with.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.840
I imagine a lot of you are probably expecting me&nbsp;
to say something about how awful that is and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.960
grinding the benchmarks is bad for improvement&nbsp;
and, it certainly can be, but I don't think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.440
has to be. The voltaic benchmarks have continued&nbsp;
to be iterated upon over the years but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.320
never was any question about what they were being&nbsp;
designed to do. They were to test aiming skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.160
in a way that paints the most complete picture&nbsp;
possible, something they have become very good at.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.480
Other benchmarks have been made over the&nbsp;
years too but in my experience, most of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.400
are either structurally identical to the voltaic&nbsp;
benchmarks or hyperfocused on a specific category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.400
and that's not to say that all of these are bad&nbsp;
but I wanted to try something a little different.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.360
These are the viscose benchmarks and the idea&nbsp;
they started from was "what if a benchmark was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.280
designed to be a training tool". My goal was to&nbsp;
essentially trick people into playing scenarios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.160
that are good for practice, instead of just&nbsp;
good for evaluating your own skill level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.400
What this means is that they might not be&nbsp;
as consistent as voltaic's benchmarks or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.600
cover as many areas of aim, but instead they&nbsp;
focus on the scenarios and categories that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.440
have found most useful to my own personal&nbsp;
aim development no matter what game I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.480
playing. The score targets are slapped on as&nbsp;
a motivator but they aren't really the point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.320
The goal here instead is to develop&nbsp;
strong fundamentals and good technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.800
which will transfer over to any game. You can&nbsp;
play other game specific training methods on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.720
of using these to benchmark if you'd like to as&nbsp;
well, but I have tried to cover everything here.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.160
If you just want to give them a try now theres&nbsp;
a link to the spreadsheet in the description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.080
go onto file, make a copy and your good to go! But&nbsp;
I want to address how these actually differ from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.160
voltaics benchmarks and how do those differences&nbsp;
better facilitate improving your technique?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.920
I think the biggest factor is the categorisation&nbsp;
of scenarios. Here are voltaic's, focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.240
primarily on the type of aim involved with even&nbsp;
numbers of scenarios for each type. The thing is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.800
the main three types of aim are not&nbsp;
equally important for in game translation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.680
and some very important scenario types&nbsp;
like pokeball are absent here since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.120
they don't really fit into any of these&nbsp;
categories, plus in my opinion target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.240
switching is fairly overrepresented.&nbsp;
What unique skill does driftts train?

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.480
What I've done instead is distribute&nbsp;
the scenarios unevenly based on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.160
I personally have found the most useful for in&nbsp;
game translation and split those scenarios into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.480
more technique focused categories. So instead&nbsp;
of categorising tasks based on what they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:06.000
I group them based on what they do. There&nbsp;
is no "target switching" category anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.240
those scenarios all fall under "flicking&nbsp;
technique" and are subdivided into which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.680
area of your flicks they focus on. Same with&nbsp;
control tracking being split into which muscle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.040
group the scenario targets. This both gives&nbsp;
you an idea of how to approach each scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.320
as well as what area your weak at if you are&nbsp;
struggling with specific ones. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.520
a lower relative score on the fingertip&nbsp;
subcategory in control tracking doesn't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.920
tell you general info like "work on tracking", it&nbsp;
is very specifically a weakness with fine control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.760
over small movements, either with your fingertips&nbsp;
or wrist depending on grip and playstyle.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.840
And while I have designed the benchmarks in a&nbsp;
way where grinding the scenarios you are weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.480
at will help address those weaknesses, if you&nbsp;
want to vary your practice more you can pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.920
out extra scenarios to help isolate the issue.&nbsp;
How I like to do this is just searching for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.360
name of the base scenario to find variants.&nbsp;
For example cloverrawcontrol, typing that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.480
brings up heaps of versions of the scenarios with&nbsp;
slight variations like slower, faster, larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.600
adding blinks and each change will help work on&nbsp;
different things. You can also check out other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.240
benchmarks on this website that have more narrow&nbsp;
focuses or look through playlists or voltaics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:25.920
recommended scenario list for tasks that will&nbsp;
help you improve at areas you are weak in.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.400
About playlists though, I've heard that a lot&nbsp;
of people play these benchmarks as if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.920
were one and this isn't something I recommend&nbsp;
personally. You can if you'd like and I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.800
it's still beneficial but in my opinion the&nbsp;
value in benchmarks is that they make it easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:47.200
for you to motivate yourself to push yourself to&nbsp;
improve category by category. How I approach every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.960
task is a test of optimisation, what am I doing&nbsp;
wrong from a technique perspective that this task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.720
is forcing me to improve on. The most recent&nbsp;
example for me was pasu, I wasn't leading my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.840
shots properly and trying to push my score&nbsp;
further forced me to improve my technique.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.040
This is, to me, what makes benchmarks better than&nbsp;
playlists and why I chose to make this list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.720
scenarios into one instead of just posting it as&nbsp;
a playlist. By encouraging you to look at your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.160
score on each scenario, set a goal and figure&nbsp;
out what is preventing you, technique wise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.754
from reaching it is why I think benchmarks have&nbsp;
become such a good tool to improve and, until now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.754 --> 00:05:26.640
have only been held back by being created to be&nbsp;
benchmarks. It prevents you from autopiloting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.040
and forces you to actively analyse your own&nbsp;
improvement which is exactly what I wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.760
when making these and dictated every decision&nbsp;
from subcategory names to rank difficulty.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:43.360
On that note, the most common question I've been&nbsp;
asked about this benchmark is why x rank is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.280
hard if it is on the easier benchmarks. A lot of&nbsp;
people have told me that fuchsia is way way harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:55.600
than wool, even though fuchsia is classified&nbsp;
as medium and wool is hard and they are right!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.160
This is an intentional design change from voltaic&nbsp;
and actually something I suggested to them during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.280
the development of season 5. I think the best&nbsp;
way to think of it is that these terms, easier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:11.200
medium and hard, represent the difficulty of&nbsp;
the scenarios included in these benchmarks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.440
NOT the skill required for any given&nbsp;
rank on those benchmarks. There will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.680
be players who I would consider advanced&nbsp;
who can't get the top rank on the medium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.800
scenarios here and that isn't just to make&nbsp;
these benchmarks really hard for no reason.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.280
The voltaic benchmarks have a bit of a problem&nbsp;
where the jumps between difficulties is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.160
drastic that it feels completely impossible to&nbsp;
broach for a lot of people. When trying to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.240
from master to grandmaster you need to not&nbsp;
only get a better score but get that score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:43.200
on a significantly harder scenario. I think&nbsp;
this is why master became cited as the cutoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.840
point for when aimtrainers lose their value&nbsp;
even though this isn't true in my opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.160
it's just that the time investment required&nbsp;
per rank grows so much after this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.120
partially because you have to get those scores&nbsp;
on incredibly difficult scenarios. What I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.400
done to try and alleviate this issue is to have&nbsp;
roughly 2 ranks of overlap for each difficulty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.520
So ordering the ranks in terms of how hard they&nbsp;
are supposed to be would look something like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.440
This means that while someone is climbing to&nbsp;
the higher ranks on the medium scenarios for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.920
example they can try out the harder ones&nbsp;
and still have scores to shoot for there.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.320
In my opinion less challenging scenarios always&nbsp;
have their place when aimtraining no matter how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.200
good you get. I think of easier scenarios&nbsp;
as refinement of your technique and harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:31.520
scenarios as a stress test of that technique,&nbsp;
you really should be playing both and this isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.240
something conventional benchmarks encourage.&nbsp;
Once you are done with intermediate on voltaic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.320
you move on to advanced. Whereas here it's&nbsp;
highly encouraged to keep playing the set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.360
of benchmarks below the highest set you can get&nbsp;
scores on. I'm not too far off silk complete on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.080
these and I still get a lot of value from&nbsp;
playing the medium difficulty scenarios.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:55.600
This is also a part of the reason why there are so&nbsp;
many ranks here, but not the only reason. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.560
goal I had with these was to make starting out&nbsp;
on them more approachable than voltaic, I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.280
most people who are brand new to aimtraining to&nbsp;
be able to get a rank in a couple of tries. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.040
means that there are a lot more ranks for&nbsp;
the easier scenarios than voltaic has, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.160
since medium overlaps with the easier and harder&nbsp;
scenarios there is a bunch of ranks there too.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.800
As for the ranks themselves, I've strayed away&nbsp;
from voltaic's contemporary energy system and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:23.680
went back to their old system of requiring&nbsp;
one score per subcategory to get a rank. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.480
reasoning for this is twofold: making people&nbsp;
play a wider range of scenarios will naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.520
lead to them become more well rounded and it also&nbsp;
allows the individual score targets to be lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:38.880
for each. Since you can get a rank in voltaic&nbsp;
with only 3-4 scores at that rank in total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.925
the individual scores are going to be a grind.&nbsp;
Whereas here, you need AT LEAST 14 scores at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.925 --> 00:08:48.720
certain rank to achieve that rank, meaning even&nbsp;
if I want fuchsia to be equal to voltaic nova as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:54.480
a rank, the fuchsia score targets can be much more&nbsp;
reachable which I personally find more motivating.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.080
I think that's everything I have to say about&nbsp;
the benchmarks themselves, but if you'd like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.320
to know more about how to play each scenario&nbsp;
specifically I'm working on adding these little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:06.080
notes to each scenario when you click it with&nbsp;
a link to a vod as well as a small writeup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.400
and if you want more information I should&nbsp;
have a video up on my second channel by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:14.560
time this goes live talking through similar&nbsp;
things with my own and mattys gameplay to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.440
demonstrate what proper technique looks like&nbsp;
for each. Also, thank you so much to pinguefy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.240
for making the spreadsheet for this. A lot of&nbsp;
my stupid ideas like bringing back volts or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:29.200
having seemingly random numbers of scenarios per&nbsp;
subcategory would not have worked at all using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.800
a benchmark template and I love the rank names&nbsp;
and colour scheme which are all pingus doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:40.160
please show them your support in the&nbsp;
comments and thanks for watching. 
 

